 ],
 "article": {
  "title": "The Power of a Good Night's Sleep",
  "topic": "健康科普（睡眠科学）",
  "level": "CET-4",
  "words": 326,
  "paras": [
   "Most people treat sleep as something that simply happens between busy days. In fact, sleep is one of the most active periods for the brain and the body. While you rest, your brain clears away waste products, your muscles repair themselves, and your memory is strengthened. Scientists now describe sleep as a nightly health check that no medicine can replace.",
   "Yet many of us do not get enough of it. A recent survey found that over half of young adults sleep less than seven hours a night. The reasons are familiar: late-night screens, heavy workloads, and the constant pull of social media. The short-term cost is easy to notice — poor concentration, irritability, and a slower reaction time. The long-term cost is more serious: repeated sleep loss is linked to a higher risk of heart disease, obesity, and anxiety.",
   "The good news is that sleep quality can be improved with simple habits. Experts suggest keeping a fixed schedule, even at weekends, and making the bedroom dark, cool, and quiet. They also advise avoiding coffee and heavy meals late in the evening. Perhaps most importantly, they recommend treating screens as the enemy of bedtime: the blue light from phones delays the release of melatonin, the hormone that makes us sleepy.",
   "Think of sleep as an investment rather than a waste of time. A well-rested person learns faster, works more steadily, and handles pressure with greater ease. For students facing exams, for workers under deadlines, and for anyone who wants to stay healthy, the nightly routine may be the single most powerful habit they can build."
